# Memory-corruption instrumentation for #72, and the physical-memory access that
# settled a four-day-old wrong belief about this device.
#
# READ THIS FIRST: PSTORE WORKS. IT HAS WORKED ALL ALONG.
#
# This fragment was written to answer "does this SoC preserve DRAM across a
# reset, and if not, what is the capture path for #72's oops?" — because the
# repo had recorded, in several places and as settled fact, that it does not:
#
#     "pstore cannot help because this SoC's reset does not preserve DRAM
#      (ramoops IS registered and /sys/fs/pstore is empty after every crash)"
#
# Both halves are wrong, measured on 2026-08-27 with build #96:
#
#   * DRAM survives a WDT_SWRST intact. Magics planted at thirteen reserved
#     physical addresses were read back byte-exact after a full reset and
#     bootloader run — including five planted INSIDE ramoops@44410000 itself.
#     The negative control worked too: lk@46000000 came back holding ARM code
#     (0xea000007), because LK really is reloaded there, which is what a lost
#     region looks like. See scripts/gemini-dram-persistence.sh.
#   * /sys/fs/pstore is empty because **systemd-pstore.service empties it**. It
#     is enabled by default on Debian, runs at every boot, and MOVES every
#     record out of /sys/fs/pstore into /var/lib/systemd/pstore/ before any
#     human looks. The records were on the device the whole time: eighteen
#     dmesg-ramoops files going back to 2026-08-21, with full register dumps and
#     complete Call traces — the exact artefact #72 has been called
#     uncapturable for lack of.
#
# The end-to-end positive control: a magic string written to /dev/pmsg0, one
# gemini-reboot, and the same string read back out of
# /var/lib/systemd/pstore/pmsg-ramoops-0. Nothing is broken.
#
# So look in /var/lib/systemd/pstore/, not /sys/fs/pstore/. That is the whole
# fix, and it costs nothing.
#
# WHAT THIS FRAGMENT IS STILL FOR
#
# PAGE_POISONING — report corruption while the machine is still healthy.
#
# slub_debug=FZPU was tried in build #92 and caught nothing across eight CONSYS
# power cycles, which with red-zoning on argues the corruption is not a slab API
# misuse. But slub_debug only watches slab objects, and nothing has ever watched
# ordinary free pages. "A write into a page that was freed back to the buddy
# allocator" fits the evidence: invisible to slub_debug, produces a wild pointer
# in whatever gets that page next, and only shows up in code that walks every
# cached object (sync, drop_caches) rather than at the moment of the write.
#
# PAGE_POISONING fills freed pages with a pattern and verifies it on the next
# allocation. A mismatch prints "pagealloc: memory corruption" with the offset
# and a hex dump, to dmesg, from a healthy machine — so unlike an oops it does
# not depend on any capture path at all. It names WHAT was written and WHERE in
# the page, a signature that can be matched against a driver.
#
# Enabled by CONFIG_PAGE_POISONING alone on 6.6 (mm/page_poison.c is gated on a
# static key set in init_mem_debugging_and_hardening(), no boot parameter),
# provided init_on_free is off — this kernel reports "heap free:off".
#
# COST: a memset of every page on free and a compare on every alloc. Real, but
# far cheaper than DEBUG_PAGEALLOC, which unmaps free pages and would fault the
# WRITER directly. DEBUG_PAGEALLOC is the next instrument now that the capture
# path is known to work; it was not reachable before, because a fault it caught
# would still have had to be read somewhere.
#
# STRICT_DEVMEM off — read physical memory.
#
# With it on, `busybox devmem 0x44410000` returns "mmap: Operation not
# permitted": /dev/mem is restricted to IO space, which is why gemini-reboot's
# watchdog pokes work and nothing else does. CONFIG_PROC_KCORE is off and would
# be read-only anyway, so this is the only route, and without it none of the
# measurements above could have been made — the persistence question would still
# be open and pstore would still be blamed on the hardware.
#
# It does weaken /dev/mem on a machine that is somebody's daily driver. It is a
# deliberate LAB SETTING on a development target reachable only over a USB
# gadget and a tailnet. Drop it once the physical-memory questions are closed;
# scripts/gemini-dram-persistence.sh says so in its own header too.
#
# #72 IS ATTRIBUTED (2026-08-28) and it was never a memory bug: the recurring
# "wild-pointer oops" is the watchdog reset producing a spurious data abort as
# the SoC comes apart. Two devmem writes alone reproduce it 4/4; the entire
# gemini-reboot minus those two writes reproduces it 0/3. So PAGE_POISONING and
# DEBUG_PAGEALLOC were watching for a write that does not exist, and they are
# off again. DEBUG_PAGEALLOC has to be turned off EXPLICITLY -- merge_config
# adds symbols but never removes one already in .config, so deleting the
# fragment silently left it enabled in #105.
#
# CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM stays off. It is not a #72 diagnostic: it is what lets
# scripts/gemini-dram-persistence.sh and any /dev/mem read of RAM work at all,
# and this session used it again to read the CONSYS EMI control block. Drop it
# when the physical-memory questions are closed, not before.
# Only DEBUG_PAGEALLOC itself: with it off, DEBUG_PAGEALLOC_ENABLE_DEFAULT has
# no Kconfig dependency satisfied and does not appear in .config at all, so
# asking for it as "is not set" is a line the merge legitimately drops --
# gemini-kbuild.sh caught exactly that and refused to build, which is what it is
# for.
# CONFIG_PAGE_POISONING is not set
# CONFIG_DEBUG_PAGEALLOC is not set
# CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM is not set
